(728890, 76032, 19035, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by lindsay:</b></div><hr/><i> I voted - and commented - on this photograph already, but I wanted to come back to it. This was one of my favorite photos, though I was pretty sure you were going to get some of the comments like "does not fit the challenge." Phooey, I say! I think the focus on the flower and blur of the weeds only adds MORE opposition. Now we have flower/weeds AND focused/unfocused. And anyway, people really do tend to dote on the flower more than the weed. It really shows peoples views on each element: they adore the flower and ignore the weeds. Anyway. I love it. I think that the "rule of thirds" would ruin this shot. Gorgeous gorgeous work.. blows mine out of the water.<br/> <br/>Thank you so much for your comments - your thoughts were exactly what I was going for. Thanks again.<br/> <br/>Those are my two cents, anyway (or was it two dollars by now? I''ll stop rambling.) </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Originally posted by lindsay: I voted - and commented - on this photograph already, but I wanted to come back to it. This was one of my favorite photos, though I was pretty sure you were going to get some of the comments like "does not fit the challenge." Phooey, I say! I think the focus on the flower and blur of the weeds only adds MORE opposition. Now we have flower/weeds AND focused/unfocused. And anyway, people really do tend to dote on the flower more than the weed. It really shows peoples views on each element: they adore the flower and ignore the weeds. Anyway. I love it. I think that the "rule of thirds" would ruin this shot. Gorgeous gorgeous work.. blows mine out of the water. Thank you so much for your comments - your thoughts were exactly what I was going for. Thanks again. Those are my two cents, anyway (or was it two dollars by now? I''ll stop rambling.) ', '2004-05-20 12:08:36.000000', NULL, 0),
